Clustering, sort keys, and zone maps.

Columnar storage means you read only the columns you need. Data skipping goes further — it lets you avoid reading most of the rows, too, and without an index. This is how columnar MPP replaces B-trees.

Start with zone maps. Warehouses split each column into blocks, and store lightweight metadata per block — most importantly, the minimum and maximum value in that block. That metadata is called a zone map. So when a query filters, the engine checks each block's min and max, and reads only the blocks whose range could contain a match — skipping the rest. Take a filter: where order date equals the twelfth of April, twenty twenty-six. A block whose values run from twenty-twenty to twenty twenty-one? Skip it. A block from January to June twenty twenty-six? Read it. A block from twenty twenty-seven? Skip it. In Snowflake, this comes from micro-partitions — data auto-split into small immutable partitions, each carrying these per-column stats. In Redshift and BigQuery, it's zone maps, or block-range — the same min-max-per-block idea.

But — and this is the catch — zone maps only help if related values sit together, in few blocks. If the dates are scattered, then every block's min-max spans everything, and nothing can be skipped. So you physically order the data by the columns you filter on. On Redshift, that's a sort key — you store the table sorted by, say, order date. On Snowflake and BigQuery, that's clustering — auto-clustering, or cluster-by — keeping data grouped by chosen columns. Cluster on order date, and all of April twenty twenty-six sits in a handful of micro-partitions, with tight min-max — so a date filter reads just those.

And that's the mental swap. In the old world, you'd add an index on your filter columns. In the new world, you cluster or sort on the columns you filter by, and let zone maps prune. Same aim — read less — but a different mechanism, one that suits columnar MPP. So: zone maps store per-block min and max, so the engine skips blocks that can't match; and clustering and sort keys physically order the data so those blocks are tight. Together, they're columnar MPP's answer to the index — data skipping, not B-trees.
